nything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.\n - **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. At the cap the controller stops claiming and posts once on the status issue.\n - **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue while a worker job runs on it.\n-- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs and the Reaper continue. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\n+- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs and the Reaper continue. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status --